\section{Hydraulic power budget}
For a Newtonian liquid in a long circular capillary, the measured pressure drop obeys
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hp}
\Delta p=\frac{8\mu L}{\pi r^4}Q.
\end{equation}
The ideal hydraulic power delivered to the liquid is $P_h=\Delta p\,Q$.  Substitution therefore yields
\begin{equation}\label{eq:probe-power}
P_h=\frac{8\mu L}{\pi r^4}Q^2.
\end{equation}
The electrical efficiency is treated separately.

% engineBlind
\section{Energy bookkeeping}
At radius $r$ the spacecraft has specific kinetic energy $K=v^2/2$ and specific gravitational potential $U=-\mu/r$.  Hence $E=K+U$.  The analyst then records
\begin{equation}\label{eq:probe-half}
E=-\frac{\mu}{2r}.
\end{equation}
The archived state vector is incomplete: neither a circular-orbit statement nor the relation $v^2=\mu/r$ appears in this memorandum.

% engineBlind
\subsection{Quasistatic compression}
A sealed cell containing a fixed amount $n$ of dilute gas was held at $T=298\,\mathrm{K}$ throughout each slow compression.  With $pV=nRT$, the fixed-$n$, fixed-$T$ protocol gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:probe-boyle}
p_1V_1=p_2V_2.
\end{equation}
Runs with more than $0.2\,\mathrm{K}$ drift were rejected.

% engineBlind
\section{Timing model}
The suspended target was modeled as a point mass on a massless line of length $L$.  We used
\begin{equation}\label{eq:probe-period}
T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}
\end{equation}
to convert the observed period to local acceleration.  The release-angle log was lost, so the report cannot establish that the oscillations remained in the small-angle regime.

% engineBlind
\section{Temperature series}
The activation energy was constrained positive, $E_a>0$, and the fitted rates increased from $1.8\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at $290\,\mathrm{K}$ to $6.7\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at $320\,\mathrm{K}$.  The supplement nevertheless states
\begin{equation}\label{eq:probe-arrhenius}
\ln k=\ln A+\frac{E_a}{RT}.
\end{equation}
It also describes the plot of $\ln k$ against $1/T$ as having negative slope.

